Alfre meal has brought out a new  facelift version of the Giulietta it's  closely aligned to the styling of the  flagship Giulia with a more sporty look  especially at the front  oh man Wow Schafer says the car he's  testing is powered by a 1.6 liter turbo  diesel engine with 88 kilowatts or 120  horsepower its combined with the alpha  twin clutch transmission so it's an  automatic but you also have shift  paddles on the steering wheel so if you  want an even sporty ride you can shift  the gears manually we tested the new  engine which is available for the first  time in the Giulietta all in all there  are five gasoline and three diesel  versions these range in power from 77 to  177 kilowatts the darker headlights  surrounds help underline the overall  sporty-er look along with the rear door  handles which are set into the window  frames  the cockpit has an uncluttered and  sporty look air conditioning is included  even in the basic version the newly  upgraded infotainment system is largely  controlled by touchscreen but it does  have a few knobs for key functions too  there are also buttons on the steering  wheel for the onboard entertainment  system and Bluetooth even though the  cabin looks great there are very few  storage options there's a pocket in the  left hand door you could just about get  a very small bottle in there otherwise  only small objects and here you could  get a coffee cup in otherwise everything  down there is taken up with USB and SD  card connections and the drive mode  switch and B space in the back but it's  just covered with plastic what a shame  the Alfa Giulietta diesel can reach 100  kilometers in 10.2 seconds and has a top  speed of 195 km/h Alfa says average fuel  consumption is 3.9 liters per 100  kilometers  the manual says the Giulietta has three  drive modes known as DNA dynamic natural  and all-weather dynamic is the sporty  option of course natural is for  fuel-efficient everyday use and  all-weather is for poor driving  conditions so how does it feel  mono has just selected a dynamic drive  mode and immediately notices the  difference the gas pedal responds a lot  faster the steering is heavier so you  get better feedback from the road and  when you accelerate the gears shift  later joining the braking system also  changes depending on the drive mode and  that's real mode everything is more  sedate the gas is very smooth the gears  change at the earliest possible moment  that makes the Giulietta pretty quiet  especially considering it's a diesel so  its pleasant to drive and the steering  is quite a bit softer thanks to the  electric power steering  amano l can hardly tell the difference  between natural and all-weather mode  that's because the gas response and  steering are identical in both modes the  only difference is the addition of ESC  and ASR stability and traction control  systems which support the driver unload  traction surfaces not that you can drive  off-road of course decimal number in  scale in the phone cut the Giulietta is  certainly not designed for that  it feels most at home on country roads  with plenty of bends all in all mano l  is impressed with the Giulietta the cool  exterior design the smart interior the  engine in combination with the automatic  transmission which works really well  there's just one thing he finds strange  the on-board computer shows the average  fuel consumption and kilometers per  litre does that mean drivers have to  work out for themselves how many litres  induces per 100 kilometers which is  standard on all other European cars  prices for the Giulietta started 21,000  400 euros in Germany
 

